<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marxism–Leninism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Marxism-Leninism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>–Leninism is the communist ideology developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> and codified under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> in the late 1920s. It holds that a vanguard party should seize power, rule a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> in the name of the working class and build socialism through state ownership and central planning. It was the official ideology of the Soviet Union and of most communist states of the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism.jpg" alt="Marxism–Leninism" width="320" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustavs Klucis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Klucis,_%D0%92%D1%8B%D1%88%D0%B5_%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D1%8F_%D0%9C%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B0,_%D0%AD%D0%BD%D0%B3%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D0%B0,_%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B8_%D0%A1%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0!.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>–Leninism drew on Bolshevism, Leninism and classical Marxism. It began as the practical politics of the Bolshevik faction of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> turned into a vanguard of professional revolutionaries who chose leaders and policy by democratic centralism. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> seized power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917 and fought the White Movement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. In the 1917 election to the Constituent Assembly they won 23.3 percent of the vote against 37.6 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-revolutionary-party">Socialist Revolutionary Party</a>, and the assembly was dissolved in January 1918.</p><p>After Lenin's death on 21 January 1924, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> and his supporters gained control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>–Leninism became a distinct movement. It rejected the view of Western Marxists that world revolution was needed first, in favour of socialism in one country. By the late 1920s Stalin had established ideological orthodoxy in the party and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>–Leninism holds that a two-stage communist revolution must replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>. A vanguard party, organised through democratic centralism, seizes power on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> and establishes a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>, which controls the means of production and suppresses opposition to pave the way for a classless and stateless society. Its worldview is atheist, and it supports universal welfare, with healthcare and education provided by the state. Its approach to international relations rests on Lenin's essay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism-the-highest-stage-of-capitalism">Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</a> of 1917.</p><p>From 1928 Stalin's five-year plans brought rapid industrialisation, concentrated on heavy industry, and the collectivisation of agriculture, which stood at 23.6 percent in 1930 and 98.0 percent in 1941. After Stalin's death it was revised into Titoism, Guevarism, Ho Chi Minh Thought, Hoxhaism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maoism">Maoism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>–Leninism was the state ideology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a>. The union <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a>, formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1980, was the first trade union in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> not controlled by a Marxist–Leninist party, and the Eastern European governments and the Soviet Union fell by 1991.</p><p>Critics from across the political spectrum have attacked it, above all in its Stalinist and Maoist forms. Historians such as Silvio Pons and David Martin Walker connect killings and repression of dissidents and social classes, as in the Red Terror and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, partly to the ideology, while others offer different explanations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a> rejected the vanguard idea, and some left-wing critics see it as state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marxism–Leninism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Soviet economy was organised on <b>Marxist-Leninist</b> principles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Brezhnev chose compromise; on 3 August the Bratislava Declaration affirmed fidelity to <b>Marxism-Leninism</b> and the Soviet Union's intention to intervene if a &quot;bourgeois&quot; system was ever established.</blockquote><a href="/movement/prague-spring">Prague Spring · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>After Lenin’s death <b>Marxism–Leninism</b>, resting on dialectical materialism, the leading role of the party and a planned economy with industrialisation and collectivisation, became the official ideology of Communist parties; at its height it covered at least a third of the world’s population.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Hwang rediscovered the speech in the late 1950s, when Kim Il Sung, having established a cult of personality, sought to develop his own version of <b>Marxism–Leninism</b> and solidify his position in the Workers' Party of Korea.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/juche">Juche · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>From within <b>Marxism–Leninism</b>, Enver Hoxha of Albania denounced Deng as a revisionist in 1978 and attacked Maoism itself, arguing that New Democracy halts class struggle, that the Three Worlds Theory is “counter-revolutionary”, and questioning Mao’s guerrilla methods.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Lenin’s death in 1924 opened a struggle between Stalin and Trotsky over the interpretation of Marxist and Leninist theory, and this period is marked by the development of <b>Marxism–Leninism</b> as the dominant strain.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/marxism">Marxism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
